 If you have any thoughts or any questions, I have already done that.

49
00:11:41,820 --> 00:11:59,100
 On the SQL days, we designed by tidying up, tidying up basically every fact in one place

50
00:11:59,100 --> 00:12:02,400
 and join when we need it.

51
00:12:02,400 --> 00:12:05,690
 MongoDB turns it around a bit.

52
00:12:05,690 --> 00:12:11,560
 First you ask basically, how will my app use this data?

53
00:12:11,560 --> 00:12:14,780
 What does it read together?

54
00:12:14,780 --> 00:12:22,990
 And then you shape the document so that one read gives you what you need.

55
00:12:22,990 --> 00:12:31,420
 So the rule of thumb here, data that is read together is stored together.

56
00:12:31,420 --> 00:12:35,740
 So let's make this maybe a little bit more concrete.

57
00:12:35,740 --> 00:12:42,110
 So what does our web shop app here show most often?

58
00:12:42,110 --> 00:12:46,730
 What could it be?

59
00:12:46,730 --> 00:12:51,160
 Well, it's probably the product page or the shopping cart

60
00:12:51,160 --> 00:12:54,780
 or maybe the customer's order history and receipt.

61
00:12:54,780 --> 00:13:01,690
 For each of those, we want as few reads as possible.

62
00:13:01,690 --> 00:13:06,080
 So the receipt is a great example.

63
00:13:06,080 --> 00:13:13,070
 It always shows the order and all of its items together.

64
00:13:13,070 --> 00:13:15,520
 So we store them together.

65
00:13:15,520 --> 00:13:26,690
 In SQL, the design question was, what are the things and how are they related?

66
00:13:26,690 --> 00:13:33,180
 In MongoDB, on the other hand, we have the second question.

67
00:13:33,180 --> 00:13:35,990
 Second question, not within us.

68
00:13:36,890 --> 00:13:40,470
 How will they be used?

69
00:13:40,470 --> 00:13:50,300
 So two apps with exactly the same data could have different MongoDB designs.

70
00:13:50,300 --> 00:13:53,120
 Because they read the data differently.

71
00:13:53,120 --> 00:14:01,890
 And that might feel strange after these SQL days we had, where there was one correct,

72
00:14:01,890 --> 00:14:03,450
 normalized design.

73
00:14:03,450 --> 00:14:08,660
 In MongoDB, design is more about trade-offs, actually.

74
00:14:08,660 --> 00:14:14,270
 Embed or reference?

75
00:14:14,270 --> 00:14:16,990
 You have two choices for related data.

76
00:14:16,990 --> 00:14:25,490
 Embed means you put it inside the document, like the items inside an order.

77
00:14:25,490 --> 00:14:30,230
 Reference means you just store the ID of another document.

78
00:14:30,830 --> 00:14:34,270
 Like, for example, the customer_id in the order.

79
00:14:34,270 --> 00:14:37,020
 That's like a foreign key.

80
00:14:37,020 --> 00:14:39,430
 You can think of it as a foreign key.

81
00:14:39,430 --> 00:14:41,790
 But MongoDB doesn't check it for you.

82
00:14:41,790 --> 00:14:44,270
 Okay?

83
00:14:44,870 --> 00:14:49,930
 So think of embedding like a folder with all of the papers inside it.

84
00:14:49,930 --> 00:14:56,370
 Everything about the order is right there when you open the folder.

85
00:14:56,370 --> 00:14:58,020
 Okay?

86
00:14:58,020 --> 00:15:05,640
 But referencing is like a note that says, see the customer's file, number one or whatever.

87
00:15:07,760 --> 00:15:12,360
 So you have to go and get the other folder.

88
00:15:12,360 --> 00:15:18,260
 So embedding is faster to read because it's one trip to the database.

89
00:15:18,900 --> 00:15:25,790
 Referencing keeps each piece of data in one place so it's easier to keep correct.

90
00:15:25,790 --> 00:15:32,670
 And one important difference from SQL as well is MongoDB doesn't check references for you.

91
00:15:32,670 --> 00:15:44,510
 If the order says customer ID 999 and there is no customer 999, MongoDB won't stop you.

92
00:15:44,510 --> 00:15:47,860
 There are no foreign key constraints here.

93
00:15:47,860 --> 00:15:51,130
 So the app here has to be careful.

94
00:15:58,270 --> 00:16:03,500
 So when do we embed and when do we actually reference?

95
00:16:03,500 --> 00:16:06,420
 Well, here's a simple checklist.

96
00:16:06,420 --> 00:16:16,990
 Embed when you always read it together with the parent, when it belongs only to that parent.

97
00:16:16,990 --> 00:16:25,820
 And also when there aren't too many and when it doesn't change that much as well.

98
00:16:25,820 --> 00:16:36,640
 Reference when many documents share the same thing.

99
00:16:36,640 --> 00:16:42,640
 Like a customer who has many orders or when a list could grow forever.

100
00:16:42,640 --> 00:16:51,420
 Okay, let's maybe test the checklist on a few examples here.

101
00:16:51,420 --> 00:16:59,630
 So let's see if this is an embed or a reference.

102
00:16:59,630 --> 00:17:02,940
 A blog post and its comments.

103
00:17:02,940 --> 00:17:09,710
 That's usually embed if there are few comments.

104
00:17:09,710 --> 00:17:16,890
 But if a post can get thousands of reference, then that's another thing.

105
00:17:16,890 --> 00:17:24,490
 Let's just say a student and their courses.

106
00:17:24,490 --> 00:17:28,070
 That's also a reference.

107
00:17:28,070 --> 00:17:33,690
 Many students share the same course and the course details change.

108
00:17:33,690 --> 00:17:37,260
 Maybe a person and their address.

109
00:17:37,260 --> 00:17:42,250
 Could that be a person and the address?

110
00:17:42,250 --> 00:17:43,930
 That would be maybe embed.

111
00:17:43,930 --> 00:17:50,370
 It belongs to that person and is read together with them.

112
00:17:50,370 --> 00:17:53,410
 Let's take another example.

113
00:17:53,410 --> 00:17:55,330
 A product and its reviews.

114
00:17:56,400 --> 00:17:57,680
 That actually depends.

115
00:17:58,890 --> 00:18:01,660
 A few reviews per product.

116
00:18:01,660 --> 00:18:04,840
 Maybe embed thousands.

117
00:18:04,840 --> 00:18:06,680
 If it's thousands then reference.

118
00:18:07,830 --> 00:18:10,770
 The answer is often actually it depends.

119
00:18:10,770 --> 00:18:11,710
 And that's fine.

120
00:18:11,710 --> 00:18:18,040
 The point is to think about the four questions maybe on this slide.

121
00:18:18,040 --> 00:18:26,050
 I mean read together one owner how many and how often it changes.

122
00:18:26,880 --> 00:18:28,400
 Or it changes.

123
00:18:28,400 --> 00:18:41,830
 So our webshop design here.

124
00:18:44,880 --> 00:18:48,980
 Basically this is how our webshop is designed.

125
00:18:48,980 --> 00:18:51,540
 Items are embedded in orders.

126
00:18:51,540 --> 00:18:54,110
 The customer is a reference.

127
00:18:54,110 --> 00:18:57,120
 Products are their own collection.

128
00:18:57,120 --> 00:19:03,030
 And if we look for example at the last row.

129
00:19:03,030 --> 00:19:07,190
 The product's name and price are copied into each other.

130
00:19:08,310 --> 00:19:10,750
 Into each order item.

131
00:19:10,750 --> 00:19:13,280
 And that's actually on purpose.

132
00:19:13,280 --> 00:19:16,160
 Just like unit_price in SQL.

133
00:19:16,160 --> 00:19:22,560
 If the hoodie gets more expensive tomorrow.

134
00:19:22,560 --> 00:19:26,050
 Old receipts must still show the old price.

135
00:19:26,050 --> 00:19:35,660
 So maybe let's walk through each row and give the reason in plain words.

136
00:19:35,660 --> 00:19:37,050
 Order items.

137
00:19:37,050 --> 00:19:43,170
 You never look at an order item without its order.

138
00:19:43,170 --> 00:19:49,100
 And the item never changes basically after the order is placed.

139
00:19:50,030 --> 00:19:52,190
 So it lives inside the order.

140
00:19:54,430 --> 00:19:56,340
 Customer.

141
00:19:56,340 --> 00:19:58,900
 One customer can have hundreds of orders.

142
00:19:59,530 --> 00:20:01,370
 And if Anna here moves.

143
00:20:03,070 --> 00:20:06,430
 We want to change her city in one place.

144
00:20:06,430 --> 00:20:09,570
 So the order only keeps her ID.

145
00:20:09,570 --> 00:20:15,950
 And third we have products.

146
00:20:15,950 --> 00:20:24,710
 You look basically at products on their own all of the time.

147
00:20:26,440 --> 00:20:28,570
 On the product page and search.

148
00:20:28,570 --> 00:20:32,610
 And maybe stock changes with every sale.

149
00:20:32,610 --> 00:20:36,290
 So they have their own collection.

150
00:20:36,290 --> 00:20:42,400
 And they copied name and price in the items.

151
00:20:42,400 --> 00:20:44,220
 Last one here.

152
00:20:44,860 --> 00:20:48,540
 Those are basically snapshots of what the customer bought.

153
00:20:48,540 --> 00:20:50,050
 At that price.

154
00:20:50,050 --> 00:20:51,010
 On that day.

155
00:20:51,010 --> 00:20:57,560
 So notice that this design is basically what we did in SQL.

156
00:20:57,560 --> 00:21:03,970
 But with order_items moved inside orders.

157
00:21:03,970 --> 00:21:08,190
 I know this could be a little tricky to understand sometimes.

158
00:21:08,930 --> 00:21:15,540
 Hopefully it shouldn't be more complicated than this.

159
00:21:15,540 --> 00:21:17,220
 This is the last day actually.

160
00:21:20,870 --> 00:21:22,390
 I can't see if you're...

