 Hold on.

162
00:21:25,150 --> 00:21:26,010
 Okay.

163
00:21:26,010 --> 00:21:28,000
 Copies have price.

164
00:21:28,000 --> 00:21:28,560
 It says there.

165
00:21:29,360 --> 00:21:32,930
 Copying data is called denormalization.

166
00:21:32,930 --> 00:21:36,250
 It makes reading fast.

167
00:21:36,250 --> 00:21:39,390
 Because everything is already in one place.

168
00:21:39,390 --> 00:21:41,390
 But if you ask yourself.

169
00:21:41,390 --> 00:21:46,300
 Should this copy change when the original changes?

170
00:21:46,300 --> 00:21:52,940
 Price on a receipt maybe should freeze.

171
00:21:54,220 --> 00:21:56,520
 That's a good copy.

172
00:21:56,520 --> 00:22:00,670
 A customer's email should be the same everywhere.

173
00:22:00,670 --> 00:22:06,690
 I mean if we had copied it into thousands of orders.

174
00:22:06,690 --> 00:22:10,390
 Changing it would mean thousands of updates.

175
00:22:10,800 --> 00:22:14,690
 And missing one would give wrong data.

176
00:22:14,690 --> 00:22:23,930
 And that's the update problem that we had on SQL last week.

177
00:22:23,930 --> 00:22:28,210
 Let's maybe do a thought experiment here.

178
00:22:28,210 --> 00:22:29,380
 Experiment, sorry.

179
00:22:30,330 --> 00:22:37,340
 Say we had copied Anna's email into every one of her orders.

180
00:22:37,340 --> 00:22:39,300
 For convenience.

181
00:22:39,300 --> 00:22:39,540
 Okay.

182
00:22:40,830 --> 00:22:44,000
 Now Anna changes her email.

183
00:22:44,000 --> 00:22:49,730
 We must find every order with her old email and update it.

184
00:22:49,730 --> 00:22:56,220
 If our code misses one, our data disagrees with itself.

185
00:22:56,220 --> 00:22:58,120
 So which email is right?

186
00:22:58,120 --> 00:23:05,530
 And that's the update problem from normalization day I would say.

187
00:23:05,530 --> 00:23:08,800
 And MongoDB doesn't protect us from it.

188
00:23:08,800 --> 00:23:13,600
 So before you copy something, ask yourself.

189
00:23:13,600 --> 00:23:17,370
 Should this copy change when the original changes?

190
00:23:17,370 --> 00:23:24,390
 If the answer is no, like a price on a recipe, copy is perfect.

191
00:23:24,390 --> 00:23:30,400
 If the answer is yes, like an email, store it in one place and then reference it.

192
00:23:30,400 --> 00:23:33,170
 So a useful rule here.

193
00:23:33,170 --> 00:23:36,740
 Copy things that describe the moment.

194
00:23:36,740 --> 00:23:39,930
 Referent things that describe the present.

195
00:23:40,920 --> 00:23:42,210
 Basically.

196
00:23:42,210 --> 00:23:50,300
 Watch out.

197
00:23:50,300 --> 00:23:53,960
 Because arrays can actually grow forever here.

198
00:23:53,960 --> 00:23:55,100
 A classic mistake.

199
00:23:55,100 --> 00:23:59,520
 Putting all of the customer's order inside the customer document.

200
00:24:00,440 --> 00:24:01,790
 It feels natural.

201
00:24:02,490 --> 00:24:06,080
 But a loyal customer might place thousands of orders.

202
00:24:06,080 --> 00:24:09,500
 And the document grows and grows.

203
00:24:09,500 --> 00:24:14,050
 A document can be at most 16 megabytes.

204
00:24:14,050 --> 00:24:17,280
 And big documents are pretty slow.

205
00:24:17,280 --> 00:24:24,890
 So orders get their own collection with the customer ID reference.

206
00:24:24,890 --> 00:24:32,720
 I mean this one looks so natural that many beginners do it.

207
00:24:32,720 --> 00:24:34,720
 A customer has orders.

208
00:24:34,720 --> 00:24:38,170
 So let's put the orders inside the customer.

209
00:24:38,170 --> 00:24:41,980
 It works fine for the first maybe 10 orders.

210
00:24:41,980 --> 00:24:48,260
 But some customers will actually order every week for years.

211
00:24:49,370 --> 00:24:52,320
 So the document gets bigger and bigger.

212
00:24:52,320 --> 00:24:58,820
 So every time you read the customer you will get all of the orders too.

213
00:24:58,820 --> 00:25:01,500
 Even if you only wanted their name.

214
00:25:01,500 --> 00:25:08,860
 And every update rewrites a bigger and bigger document.

215
00:25:08,860 --> 00:25:12,960
 And there is a hard limit here.

216
00:25:12,960 --> 00:25:17,000
 One document can be at most 16 megabytes.

