1044
00:44:28,090 --> 00:44:33,450
 We haven't talked about injections in MongoDB.

1045
00:44:33,450 --> 00:44:38,100
 And MongoDB has no SQL so there is no SQL injections in MongoDB.

1046
00:44:38,100 --> 00:44:39,450
 Right?

1047
00:44:39,450 --> 00:44:40,900
 Not quite.

1048
00:44:40,900 --> 00:44:42,580
 Imagine a website that sends

1049
00:44:44,800 --> 00:44:47,420
 json to our python code.

1050
00:44:47,420 --> 00:44:52,370
 A normal user sends an email address.

1051
00:44:52,370 --> 00:44:55,650
 An attacker sends a small dictionary.

1052
00:44:55,650 --> 00:45:09,530
 Instead for example maybe he can say $ne null that ends up basically inside our filter.

1053
00:45:10,390 --> 00:45:13,790
 And the filter now means email is not null.

1054
00:45:13,790 --> 00:45:16,710
 And that matches every customer.

1055
00:45:16,710 --> 00:45:19,860
 And the attacker gets someone else's data.

1056
00:45:19,860 --> 00:45:21,780
 Same idea as SQL injection.

1057
00:45:22,650 --> 00:45:25,190
 User input becomes part of the query.

1058
00:45:25,190 --> 00:45:31,050
 So maybe let's walk through an attack here step by step.

1059
00:45:31,050 --> 00:45:38,690
 Our website has a form where you type your email to see your orders for example.

1060
00:45:38,690 --> 00:45:43,840
 The website sends the email to our python code as json.

1061
00:45:43,840 --> 00:45:54,430
 A normal request looks like email: anna@example.com

1062
00:45:54,430 --> 00:45:55,730
 Okay.

1063
00:45:55,730 --> 00:46:00,480
 Our code puts that value in the filter.

1064
00:46:00,480 --> 00:46:02,930
 And all is well.

1065
00:46:02,930 --> 00:46:06,080
 Now the attacker sends something else.

1066
00:46:06,080 --> 00:46:14,690
 Email colon curly bracket $ne null dot json loads.

1067
00:46:14,690 --> 00:46:18,140
 And turns that into a python dictionary.

1068
00:46:18,140 --> 00:46:22,690
 And our code puts the dictionary straight into the filter.

1069
00:46:22,690 --> 00:46:28,510
 Now the filter says find the customers whose email is not null.

1070
00:46:28,510 --> 00:46:31,310
 And every customer matches.

1071
00:46:31,310 --> 00:46:36,770
 So the attacker gets the first one without knowing any email at all.

1072
00:46:36,770 --> 00:46:42,010
 And that's called operator injection.

1073
00:46:42,010 --> 00:46:45,910
 It's MongoDB's cousin of SQL injection.

1074
00:46:46,790 --> 00:46:54,750
 And it's on lists of the most common security problems in web apps actually.

1075
00:46:54,750 --> 00:47:02,570
 So how do we protect ourselves?

1076
00:47:02,570 --> 00:47:12,890
 Well, we build the filter yourself with values you have checked.

1077
00:47:12,890 --> 00:47:15,230
 Check the type.

1078
00:47:15,230 --> 00:47:18,300
 An email must be a string, not a dictionary.

1079
00:47:18,300 --> 00:47:29,680
 Convert input with str and int and catch the errors you expect.

1080
00:47:29,680 --> 00:47:33,170
 So the user gets a friendly message instead of a crash.

1081
00:47:33,170 --> 00:47:35,130
 We want to avoid crashes here.

1082
00:47:35,130 --> 00:47:40,060
 So how do we stop it?

1083
00:47:40,060 --> 00:47:42,860
 Well, the key idea is the same as with SQL.

1084
00:47:42,860 --> 00:47:46,620
 Never let user input become part of the query itself.

1085
00:47:47,180 --> 00:47:48,300
 So rule one.

1086
00:47:48,300 --> 00:47:51,180
 Build the filter dictionary yourself in your code.

1087
00:47:52,610 --> 00:47:56,100
 User input only goes in as values.

1088
00:47:56,100 --> 00:47:57,150
 And rule two.

1089
00:47:57,150 --> 00:47:58,270
 Check the type.

1090
00:47:58,270 --> 00:48:01,090
 An email is always a string.

1091
00:48:01,090 --> 00:48:07,110
 If you get a dictionary or a list, reject it.

1092
00:48:07,930 --> 00:48:08,930
 And rule three.

1093
00:48:08,930 --> 00:48:10,320
 Convert input.

1094
00:48:10,320 --> 00:48:14,050
 Input in Python always gives a string, which is safe.

1095
00:48:14,050 --> 00:48:16,570
 But if you need a number, use int.

1096
00:48:16,570 --> 00:48:18,430
 Inside maybe a try.

1097
00:48:18,430 --> 00:48:21,930
 So the program doesn't crash on ABC.

1098
00:48:22,650 --> 00:48:24,020
 For example.

1099
00:48:24,020 --> 00:48:25,460
 Rule four.

1100
00:48:25,460 --> 00:48:29,460
 Catch the errors you expect and give a friendly message.

1101
00:48:30,250 --> 00:48:34,070
 You should see that email already registered.

1102
00:48:36,210 --> 00:48:37,410
 Not a long trace back.

